  "story": {
    "headline": "This Amazing Archive of Animal Photography Reveals 18,000 Species and Counting. Here's How Joel Sartore Built His 'Photo Ark'",
    "summary": "For 20 years, the National Geographic photographer has showcased Earth’s animals in a series of striking images that make even the tiniest creatures look immense",
    "publisher": "Rally News",
    "url": "https://rally.news/categories/arts/?article=This%2BAmazing%2BArchive%2Bof%2BAnimal%2BPhotography%2BReveals%2B18%2C000%2BSpecies%2Band%2BCounting.%2BHere%27s%2BHow%2BJoel%2BSartore%2BBuilt%2BHis%2B%27Photo%2BArk%27"
  },
  "researchBrief": "Joel Sartore’s Photo Ark project, documented in Smithsonian Magazine, has photographed over 18,000 species, with the most recent addition being the critically endangered red handfish. The project aims to document at least 25,000 species living in zoos, aquariums, and wildlife sanctuaries. Sartore’s work has been supported by the National Geographic Society, and the Photo Ark has inspired conservation efforts through education and on-the-ground projects. The earliest photos were taken at the Lincoln Children’s Zoo, and the project has since expanded globally. The Photo Ark’s mission is to inspire people to protect at-risk species before they disappear. All claims about the number of species documented and the project’s goals are corroborated by multiple sources, including Smithsonian Magazine, National Geographic, and Sartore’s official website.",
  "sources": [
    "https://www.smithsonianmag.com/science-nature/this-amazing-archive-of-animal-photography-reveals-18000-species-and-counting-heres-how-joel-sartore-built-his-photo-ark-180989282/",
    "https://news.nationalgeographic.org/20-years-18-000-species-the-national-geographic-photo-ark-celebrates-double-milestone/",
    "https://www.nationalgeographic.org/explore/programs/photo-ark",
    "https://www.joelsartore.com/photo-ark/"
  ],
